# Environment configuration for the URL Shortener.
#
# The Go application reads these once at startup through internal/config
# (config.Load) — that is the single place the process touches the
# environment. Copy this file to `.env` and adjust the values for your setup.
#
# App variables (read by internal/config): DB, REDIS_ADDR, CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS,
#   SWAGGER_HOST, PG_MAX_OPEN_CONNS
# Compose-only variables (not read by the app): GF_SECURITY_ADMIN_*

# --- Application DSNs ---
# Used by both `go run .` (local dev) and `docker compose` (containers).
# Pick hostnames according to where the app runs:
#   - localhost     : when running the Go binary directly on the host
#   - db / redis-db : when running inside docker compose (service names)
#
# The Postgres compose service exposes 5432 on the host, so `localhost` works
# from the host while the compose stack is up.
DB=postgres://postgres:secret@localhost:5432/url_shortener?sslmode=disable
REDIS_ADDR=localhost:6379

# --- Postgres connection pool (OPTIONAL) ---
# Cap on open connections PER APP INSTANCE (SetMaxOpenConns/SetMaxIdleConns in
# internal/repository/postgres/postgres.go). Default 17 is a starting point
# from the (physical_cores×2)+spindle formula — see
# docs/stress-test-findings.md — not measured for any specific host. With N
# app instances the effective total pool budget against Postgres is
# N × PG_MAX_OPEN_CONNS; keep that comfortably under Postgres max_connections.
# Tune based on go_sql_wait_count_total / go_sql_wait_duration_seconds_total
# (exported on :7070) — a rising wait count under load means this is too low.
PG_MAX_OPEN_CONNS=17

# --- CORS ---
# Comma-separated list of allowed origins for cross-origin requests.
# config.Load trims whitespace and drops duplicates/empties before handing the
# list to the HTTP transport. In production set this to your frontend
# domain(s), e.g.:
#   CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://app.example.com,https://admin.example.com
# When unset (or empty) the server falls back to localhost dev origins only.
CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=http://localhost:3000,http://localhost:8080

# --- Swagger UI host (OPTIONAL) ---
# Controls the host that Swagger UI's "Try it out" calls target.
# Leave UNSET (recommended) → relative host: swagger-ui uses window.location,
# so it works in every environment (local :8080, Docker behind Nginx :80, and
# production domains) with no per-env tuning.
# Set it only to pin an absolute host, e.g.:
#   SWAGGER_HOST=localhost:80      # Docker behind Nginx
#   SWAGGER_HOST=localhost:8080    # running the binary directly
#   SWAGGER_HOST=api.example.com   # production
# SWAGGER_HOST=

# --- Grafana admin (docker-compose.monitoring.yml) ---
# Consumed by the Grafana container, not by the Go application.
GF_SECURITY_ADMIN_USER=admin
GF_SECURITY_ADMIN_PASSWORD=admin
